# 2. AUTHENTICATION AND SECURITY

This is the highest-priority area.

Inspect the existing authentication implementation in `server.ts`, the frontend authentication context, API client and login page.

## 2.1 Implement genuine JWT authentication

The existing implementation accepts email addresses or user IDs as bearer tokens. This is insecure.

Replace this behaviour with proper signed JWT authentication.

Requirements:

- Use a maintained JWT library compatible with the existing Node.js environment.
- Generate a signed access token after successful login.
- Include the authenticated user's ID and necessary identity claims.
- Set a reasonable token expiry.
- Verify the JWT signature on every protected API request.
- Verify token expiry.
- Reject malformed, expired, tampered and invalid tokens.
- Never authenticate a user using an email address or numeric user ID directly.
- Never trust a role supplied by the frontend.
- Load the user's current role and account status from the server-side user record.
- Reject inactive or deleted users.
- Keep the existing authentication middleware and permission helper structure.
- Use environment variables for JWT secrets.
- Fail securely if the required production secret is missing.
- Never hardcode production secrets in source code.

Update the existing frontend API client so it sends the actual access token with protected requests.

Ensure login, page refresh, logout and session expiry work correctly.

## 2.2 Role-based access control

The system must support:

- ADMIN
- STAFF
- CUSTOMER

Verify permissions at the backend level.

ADMIN:
- Create and manage staff accounts.
- Manage users.
- Manage products and plans.
- Manage discount and tax configurations.
- Execute live billing.
- Access all reports and administrative information.

STAFF:
- Manage customers and their subscriptions.
- Create and manage quotations.
- Manage invoices and payments as permitted by the existing workflow.
- View operational reports.
- Cannot create administrators or staff accounts.
- Cannot execute administrator-only operations.

CUSTOMER:
- View only their own profile.
- View only their own subscriptions.
- View only their own quotations and invoices where applicable.
- View only their own payments.
- Submit requests for their own subscriptions.
- Cannot access another customer's information.
- Cannot access administrative APIs.

Check EVERY relevant API route, not just the frontend route guards.

## 2.3 Customer ownership validation

Inspect all endpoints that accept customer IDs, subscription IDs, invoice IDs and payment IDs.

A customer must never be able to access or manipulate another customer's records by changing an ID in an API request.

In particular:

- Validate subscription ownership when creating customer service requests.
- Validate invoice ownership when viewing invoice details.
- Validate payment ownership when viewing payment information.
- Validate ownership before subscription lifecycle actions.
- Do not trust customer_id sent by the frontend for customer-role requests.
- Derive customer identity from the authenticated user.

Return appropriate HTTP 401 or 403 responses.

## 2.4 Password requirements

Maintain the existing password complexity rules:

- Minimum 8 characters.
- At least one uppercase letter.
- At least one lowercase letter.
- At least one special character.

Ensure validation happens on the backend.

Use bcrypt or the existing secure password hashing implementation.

Never store plaintext passwords.

Never return password hashes in API responses.

## 2.5 Forgot password and reset password

The existing implementation generates reset tokens but does not deliver them through email and exposes the token in the API response.

Complete the existing password recovery flow.

Requirements:

1. Customer enters registered email.
2. Backend generates a cryptographically secure random token.
3. Store only a hash of the reset token.
4. Set a 15-minute expiry.
5. Send the reset link to the registered email address.
6. Do not expose the reset token in production API responses.
7. Require a valid token for every password reset.
8. Reject expired tokens.
9. Reject previously used tokens.
10. Mark the token as used after successful reset.
11. Invalidate previous reset tokens when appropriate.
12. Prevent account enumeration through consistent forgot-password responses.
13. Apply password complexity rules to the new password.
14. Record the reset event without logging the complete token.

Use a minimal email delivery integration configured through environment variables.

Do not hardcode SMTP credentials.

If email credentials are unavailable in the local development environment, provide a development-only email preview/logging mechanism that does not expose reset tokens in production.

Preserve the existing login page and its current recovery workflow. Only make the necessary changes to connect it to the real backend process.

