# 6. RECURRING BILLING ENGINE

This is one of the most important parts of the problem statement.

Inspect the existing billing simulation and execution endpoints.

Do not replace the current billing module.

Complete its existing implementation.

## 6.1 Billing simulation

Simulation must be a true dry run.

It must:

- Identify subscriptions due for billing.
- Check subscription status.
- Check expiry.
- Check next_billing_date.
- Check billing interval.
- Check applicable discounts.
- Check applicable taxes.
- Calculate invoice totals.
- Detect already-billed periods.
- Show ready, skipped and blocked subscriptions.
- Explain why a subscription is blocked or skipped.

IMPORTANT:

Simulation must not:

- Generate invoices.
- Change subscription dates.
- Update discount usage counts.
- Create payments.
- Mutate any persistent business data.

## 6.2 Correct billing eligibility

The existing execution code processes active subscriptions without adequately checking whether their next billing date is due.

Correct this.

A subscription should be billed only when:

- It is ACTIVE.
- It has reached its next billing date.
- It has not expired.
- Its plan is eligible.
- The relevant billing period has not already been processed.

Use the subscription's actual billing interval.

Do not assume that every subscription is billed monthly.

## 6.3 Billing period calculations

Implement correct period calculations for:

- Daily.
- Weekly.
- Monthly.
- Yearly.

Do not use a hardcoded 28-day billing period for all invoices.

Handle month-end dates safely.

Ensure the next billing date is advanced from the correct scheduled date rather than simply from the current execution date.

If multiple billing periods are overdue, define and implement a consistent catch-up strategy so that valid billing periods are not silently skipped.

## 6.4 Duplicate invoice prevention

The existing idempotency implementation must be hardened.

Requirements:

- Generate a unique idempotency key per subscription and billing period.
- Check the key before invoice creation.
- Prevent concurrent requests from creating duplicate invoices.
- Ensure an invoice and its idempotency record are committed consistently.
- Ensure a failed invoice creation does not leave a misleading idempotency record.
- Ensure retrying a failed request does not corrupt billing state.
- Return the existing invoice reference when the same period has already been processed.

Use the existing persistence architecture and make the smallest necessary reliability improvement.

If the current persistence mechanism cannot provide safe concurrent writes, document and implement the minimum compatible persistence improvement rather than pretending that an in-memory check is transactional.

## 6.5 Invoice generation

Each generated invoice must contain:

- Invoice number.
- Customer.
- Subscription.
- Billing period.
- Issue date.
- Due date.
- Line items.
- Quantity.
- Unit price.
- Discount.
- Tax.
- Final total.
- Paid amount.
- Outstanding balance.
- Invoice status.

Ensure calculations are consistent between simulation and actual execution.

The same input data must produce the same invoice preview and final invoice totals.

## 6.6 Automatic recurring billing

Inspect the existing auto-billing settings.

Do not leave automatic billing as a dashboard setting or a hardcoded success message.

Implement a reliable scheduled execution mechanism compatible with the existing deployment environment.

Requirements:

- Respect the configured automatic billing setting.
- Execute only due subscriptions.
- Use the same validated billing engine as manual execution.
- Prevent repeated processing of the same billing period.
- Record the result of each run.
- Handle errors without stopping unrelated subscriptions.
- Make the execution schedule configurable through environment variables or existing settings.

If the deployment platform does not support an always-running scheduler, use a compatible scheduled endpoint or platform cron integration. Do not introduce a separate application architecture.

Manual billing simulation and execution must continue to work.

